            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Bauxite Mineralogy: Genesis, Chemical Composition & Bayer Refining</h3>\n            <p><strong>Bauxite</strong> is not a single mineral species, but an amorphous, heterogeneous aluminum-rich rock that serves as the principal commercial ore for the extraction of metallic <strong>aluminum ($Al$)</strong>. Aluminum is the third most abundant element in the Earth's crust (after oxygen and silicon), but it is never found in its native metallic state due to its extreme chemical affinity for oxygen.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Chemical Composition & Mineral Constituents</h4>\n            <p>Bauxite consists predominantly of hydrated aluminum oxide minerals mixed with silica, iron oxides, and titania:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Gibbsite (Hydrargillite - $\\alpha-Al(OH)_3$ / $Al_2O_3 \\cdot 3H_2O$):</strong> Trihydrate alumina; the dominant mineral in tropical lateritic bauxites of Peninsular India. Dissolves easily in caustic soda at moderate temperatures (140°C–150°C).</li>\n                <li><strong>Boehmite ($\\gamma-AlO(OH)$ / $Al_2O_3 \\cdot H_2O$) & Diaspore ($\\alpha-AlO(OH)$):</strong> Monohydrate aluminas; harder and require higher digestion temperatures (over 240°C) and pressures.</li>\n                <li><strong>Commercial Grade Tenor:</strong> Commercial bauxite ore typically assays between <strong>45% and 55% alumina ($Al_2O_3$)</strong>, with reactive silica ($SiO_2$) kept strictly below 3% to 5% to minimize chemical reagent loss.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Geological Genesis: Tropical Lateritization</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Bauxite deposits in India are residual weathering products formed under intense tropical monsoon climates with alternating wet and dry seasons.</li>\n                <li><strong>Process:</strong> Rainwater rich in organic acids percolates through aluminous silicate rocks (khondalites, basaltic Deccan Traps, charnockites, and granitic gneisses), leaching away mobile silica, lime, and alkalis, leaving behind an insoluble residual crust of hydrated alumina and ferric oxides on high plateaus (<em>lateritic bauxite capping</em>).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Two-Stage Metallurgy: Bayer Process & Hall-Héroult Smelting</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Stage 1: The Bayer Process (Bauxite to Alumina):</strong> Raw bauxite is crushed, digested in hot concentrated Sodium Hydroxide ($NaOH$) under pressure to dissolve aluminum into soluble sodium aluminate, filtered to remove insoluble iron-rich 'Red Mud', cooled to precipitate pure Aluminum Hydroxide crystals, and calcined in rotary kilns at 1,000°C to produce pure white anhydrous <strong>Alumina powder ($Al_2O_3$)</strong>.\n                    <br><em>Input-Output Ratio: Approximately 4 to 5 tonnes of raw bauxite yield 2 tonnes of alumina.</em>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Stage 2: Hall-Héroult Electrolytic Smelting (Alumina to Aluminium):</strong> Alumina is dissolved in molten <strong>cryolite ($Na_3AlF_6$)</strong> at ~950°C and electrolyzed in carbon-lined pots with massive direct electrical currents (consuming 13,000 to 15,000 kWh per tonne of metal!).\n                    <br><em>Input-Output Ratio: 2 tonnes of alumina yield 1 tonne of pure aluminum metal.</em>\n                    <br><strong>The Golden Formula: 4 Tonnes Bauxite $\\rightarrow$ 2 Tonnes Alumina $\\rightarrow$ 1 Tonne Aluminium Metal.</strong>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
