            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Bauxite Global Landscape: World Reserves, Production Rankings & Trade</h3>\n            <p>Bauxite is an internationally traded bulk mineral that forms the base of the global multi-billion dollar aerospace, automotive, electrical, and packaging industries. Global reserves are concentrated in the tropical lateritic belts of West Africa, Oceania, and Southeast Asia.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Global Bauxite RESERVES Ranking</h4>\n            <p>According to the United States Geological Survey (USGS) and international mineral datasets, the leading nations in total geological bauxite reserves are:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Guinea &gt; Australia &gt; Vietnam &gt; Brazil &gt; Jamaica\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Guinea (West Africa - 1st in Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The undisputed global titan of bauxite reserves, holding over <strong>7.4 billion tonnes (approx. 24%–25% of the world total)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Vast plateau cappings in the <strong>Boké and Kindia regions</strong>, yielding exceptionally high-grade trihydrate gibbsite with minimal silica.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Australia (2nd in Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Holds over 5.3 billion tonnes (~17%–18% of world reserves). Concentrated in the tropical north: <strong>Weipa</strong> (Cape York Peninsula, Queensland), <strong>Gove</strong> (Northern Territory), and the <strong>Darling Range</strong> (Western Australia).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Vietnam (3rd in Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Holds massive unexploited lateritic bauxite plateaus (~5.8 billion tonnes) in the Central Highlands (Dak Nong, Lam Dong).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>4. Brazil (4th in Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Massive deposits in the Amazon Basin (Porto Trombetas in Pará state).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>India's Position:</strong> Ranks <strong>6th to 7th globally in bauxite reserves</strong> (~6% of world total).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Global Bauxite PRODUCTION Ranking</h4>\n            <p>In terms of annual commercial extraction volume, the official global hierarchy is:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Australia &gt; Guinea &gt; China &gt; Brazil &gt; India\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Australia & Guinea:</strong> Together produce over 55% of global mined bauxite, dominating international seaborne trade.</li>\n                <li><strong>China:</strong> Mines over 60–70 million tonnes domestically, but due to massive domestic smelting capacity (producing over 58% of global aluminum), China imports over 120 million tonnes of bauxite annually, chiefly from Guinea and Australia.</li>\n                <li><strong>India:</strong> Ranks <strong>5th globally in bauxite production</strong>, mining ~22 to 24 million tonnes annually to feed domestic refineries.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
