            "title": "Major Bauxite Belts of India: Panchpatmali, Gandhamardan, Amarkantak & Lohardaga",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Major Bauxite Belts of India: Geological Basins & Captive Mines</h3>\n            <p>India’s premier bauxite deposits are situated across four primary geographical clusters: the Eastern Ghats khondalite plateaus, the Central Indian Maikala-Amarkantak highlands, the Chota Nagpur western plateaus, and the Western Ghats Sahyadri laterites.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Panchpatmali Plateau (Koraput District, Odisha)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Jewel of NALCO:</strong> Panchpatmali is recognized as one of the largest single bauxite deposits in the world, stretching over a 18-km long plateau at an elevation of 1,100 to 1,300 meters.</li>\n                <li><strong>Fully Mechanized Eco-Friendly Mining:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Operated by NALCO since 1985. Features trench mining, topsoil reclamation, and simultaneous afforestation.</li>\n                        <li><strong>The 14.6-km Single-Flight Cable Belt Conveyor:</strong> In a world-renowned engineering feat, extracted bauxite is transported from the top of the Panchpatmali mountain down to the Damanjodi alumina refinery via a <strong>14.6-km curved, single-flight regenerative cable belt conveyor</strong>. The descending load drives an electrical generator, producing surplus clean electricity!</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Other Odisha Plateaus: Gandhamardan, Baphlimali & Pottangi</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Baphlimali (Rayagada):</strong> Supplies captive bauxite feed to Hindalco’s Utkal Alumina refinery via an 18-km long conveyor system.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kodingamali & Pottangi (Koraput):</strong> Developed by OMC and NALCO to ensure 50-year raw material security for Odisha refineries.</li>\n                <li><strong>Gandhamardan Hills (Bolangir/Bargarh):</strong> Massive high-grade bauxite deposit; preserved from mining due to ecological concerns and local cultural heritage (medicinal flora, Nrusinghanath and Harishankar shrines).</li>\n                <li><strong>Niyamgiri (Kalahandi/Rayagada):</strong> Rich bauxite plateau rejected for mining in 2013 following historic Gram Sabha resolutions by the indigenous <strong>Dongria Kondh</strong> tribe under the Forest Rights Act (FRA).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Central Indian Belts: Amarkantak & Maikala Range</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Spans Madhya Pradesh (Shahdol, Anuppur, Mandla) and Chhattisgarh (Surguja, Bilaspur, Kabirdham).</li>\n                <li>Forms the watershed of the Narmada and Son rivers; high-grade bauxite capping basalt and Gondwana rocks. Supplies BALCO (Korba) and Renukoot.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Chota Nagpur Plateaus: Lohardaga (Jharkhand)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The Pat lands of western Jharkhand (Bagru Hill, Pakhar, Serengdag) at 1,000 m elevation. High-grade gibbsite mined by Hindalco.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>5. Western Ghats Belt: Kolhapur & Ratnagiri (Maharashtra)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Deposits at Udgiri, Dhangarwadi, Radhanagari, and Chandgad in Kolhapur, and coastal deposits in Ratnagiri, supplying domestic refineries and coastal shipping.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
