            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Copper in India: Reserves vs Production Geography</h3>\n            <p>India is endowed with modest, low-grade geological deposits of copper ore. The domestic copper sector is marked by a striking geographical paradox: <strong>Rajasthan holds more than half of India's copper reserves, but Madhya Pradesh produces more than 60% of India's mined copper ore!</strong></p>\n\n            <h4>1. Leading States in Copper RESERVES</h4>\n            <p>According to the Indian Bureau of Mines and official geological resource inventories, the state rankings in total copper reserves are:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Rajasthan (~53.8%) &gt; Jharkhand (~19.5%) &gt; Madhya Pradesh (~18.8%)\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Rajasthan (Leader in Reserves, ~54%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Rajasthan holds the lion's share of India's total copper ore reserves (~813 million tonnes of resources).</li>\n                        <li>Distributed across the historic <strong>Khetri Copper Belt (Jhunjhunu)</strong>, <strong>Kho-Dariba (Alwar)</strong>, and <strong>Delwara-Kirowli (Udaipur)</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Jharkhand (Second in Reserves, ~19.5%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Concentrated in the famous <strong>Singhbhum Shear Zone</strong> (Mosabani, Ghatshila, Rakha, Surda, Kendadih).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Madhya Pradesh (Third in Reserves, ~18.8%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Virtually all of MP’s copper is concentrated in a single, colossal deposit: the <strong>Malanjkhand Copper Deposit (Balaghat district)</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Leading States in Copper PRODUCTION</h4>\n            <p>In terms of active annual mined copper ore, the ranking reverses entirely:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Madhya Pradesh (&gt;60% to 65%) &gt; Rajasthan (~35%) &gt; Jharkhand (~1%)\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Madhya Pradesh (Supreme Producer, >60%–65%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Driven by HCL's massive mechanized open-pit mine at <strong>Malanjkhand</strong>, which extracts over 2.5 to 3 million tonnes of copper ore annually.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Rajasthan (Second in Production, ~35%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Extracted from deep underground mines of the Khetri Copper Complex (Kolihan and Khetri mines).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Jharkhand (Minimal Output, ~1%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Many historic underground mines in Singhbhum (Mosabani, Rakha) have been closed or suspended due to high operational costs.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Low-Tenor Challenge</h4>\n            <div style=\"background:#fffbeb; border:1px solid #fde68a; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; margin-top:10px;\">\n                <strong>Indian Ore Grade:</strong> Domestic copper ores are exceptionally lean, averaging only <strong>0.8% to 1.2% Cu</strong>. In contrast, major global mines in Chile (Escondida) and Zambia mine ores grading 2% to 4% Cu. This low tenor makes domestic underground mining cost-intensive.\n            </div>\n        ",
