            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Major Copper Belts of India: Geological Clusters & Historic Mines</h3>\n            <p>India’s domestic copper resources are confined to three historical mining provinces: the Malanjkhand granitic pluton in Central India, the Aravalli-Delhi metasedimentary fold belt in Rajasthan, and the Precambrian Singhbhum Shear Zone in Jharkhand.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Malanjkhand Copper Deposit (Balaghat District, Madhya Pradesh)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>India's Largest Copper Deposit:</strong> Discovered in the 1970s; an arcuate quartz reef intruding Paleoproterozoic granitic rocks. Holds nearly <strong>70% of India's total copper ore reserves</strong> and is classified geologically as a porphyry-type copper deposit.</li>\n                <li><strong>Operations by HCL:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Operated as a colossal mechanized open-cast pit (over 2.5 km long, 1 km wide, and 200 m deep).</li>\n                        <li>HCL has completed a massive transition to <strong>underground mining</strong> to exploit deep-seated reserves below the open-pit floor, targeting 5 million tonnes per annum capacity.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Khetri Copper Belt (Jhunjhunu District, Rajasthan)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Extends for 80 km along the North Delhi Fold Belt from Singhana to Raghunathgarh. Mined since ancient times (supplying copper for the Harappan/Indus Valley Civilization).</li>\n                <li><strong>Major Producing Underground Mines:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Madhan-Kudhan (Khetri Mine):</strong> Large multi-level underground mine accessed via vertical production shafts.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Kolihan Mine:</strong> High-grade chalcopyrite veins in phyllite and quartzite.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Chandmari & Banwas:</strong> Modernized production blocks.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Other Rajasthan Copper Belts:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Kho-Dariba (Alwar district):</strong> High-grade chalcopyrite in pegmatitic quartz veins.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Delwara-Kirowli (Udaipur district)</strong> and <strong>Pur-Banera belt (Bhilwara district)</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Singhbhum Copper Belt (West & East Singhbhum, Jharkhand)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>A celebrated 130-km arcuate fault zone known as the <strong>Singhbhum Shear Zone (Copper Belt Thrust)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Historic Mining Centers:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Mosabani:</strong> India’s first commercial underground copper mine, developed by the <em>Indian Copper Corporation (ICC)</em> in 1928, descending over 1,000 meters deep.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Ghatshila (Moubhandar):</strong> Site of India’s oldest integrated copper smelter, commissioned in 1928 by ICC.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Rakha, Surda, Kendadih, and Pathargora:</strong> Famous shaft and decline mines along the thrust zone.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
