            "title": "Global Copper Landscape & India's Refined Copper Import Crisis: The Tuticorin Shock",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Global Copper Landscape & India’s Structural Refined Copper Deficit</h3>\n            <p>Copper is widely regarded by macroeconomists as <strong>'Doctor Copper'</strong> because its market demand and pricing diagnose the overall health of the global economy. India’s domestic copper sector underwent a catastrophic structural shift in 2018, converting the nation from a thriving net exporter of refined copper into a vulnerable net importer.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Global Copper Reserves & Production Rankings</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Global Copper RESERVES Ranking:</strong>\n                    <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:8px 0;\">\n                        Chile (&gt;21%) &gt; Australia (~11%) &gt; Peru (~9%) &gt; Russia (~7%) &gt; Mexico\n                    </div>\n                    <em>Chile holds over 190 million tonnes of copper reserves, anchored by the volcanic porphyry belts of the Andes Mountains.</em>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Global Copper PRODUCTION Ranking:</strong>\n                    <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:8px 0;\">\n                        Chile (&gt;25%) &gt; Peru (~10%) &gt; DRC (~10%) &gt; China (~9%) &gt; United States\n                    </div>\n                    <em>Chile produces over 5.3 million tonnes of copper annually. Giant mines: <strong>Escondida</strong> (world's largest copper mine, operated by BHP) and <strong>El Teniente</strong> (world's largest underground copper mine, operated by Codelco).</em>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. India’s Custom Smelting Model</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Because domestic mines yield only a fraction of national demand, India's large private smelters adopted the <strong>Custom Smelting Model</strong>: importing raw concentrated copper ores from Chile, Peru, Australia, and Indonesia, smelting them domestically into refined copper cathodes for domestic use and export to Asian markets.</li>\n                <li><strong>Birla Copper (Hindalco - Dahej, Gujarat):</strong> World-scale custom smelter and refinery located at Dahej port, producing over 500,000 tonnes of cathode copper annually alongside gold, silver, and DAP fertilizers. Currently India's largest operating copper smelting complex.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The 2018 Tuticorin Shock & National Supply Crisis</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Sterlite Copper Smelter (Thoothukudi / Tuticorin, Tamil Nadu):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Operated by Vedanta Limited; produced over <strong>400,000 tonnes of refined copper annually (accounting for ~40% of India's total copper output)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>In May 2018, following prolonged public protests and a tragic police firing incident over alleged sulfur dioxide and groundwater pollution, the Tamil Nadu State Government ordered the <strong>permanent closure of the Tuticorin smelter</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Economic & Strategic Fallout:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>India’s domestic refined copper production crashed by nearly <strong>50%</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>India, which had been a <strong>net exporter of refined copper (~335,000 tonnes in 2017–18)</strong>, was overnight transformed into a <strong>structural net importer (importing over 180,000 to 250,000 tonnes of copper annually)</strong>!</li>\n                        <li>Drained over ₹15,000–₹20,000 crore annually in foreign exchange and created severe supply disruptions for domestic electrical transformer and cable manufacturers.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
