            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Lead & Zinc in India: Rajasthan's National Monopoly & Rampura Agucha</h3>\n            <p>While most metallic minerals are distributed across multiple states, Lead and Zinc exhibit the most extreme geographic concentration in the Indian sub-continent: <strong>one single state—Rajasthan—holds virtually the entire national resource and accounts for 100% of primary mined production!</strong></p>\n\n            <h4>1. Rajasthan's Absolute National Monopoly</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Reserves:</strong> Rajasthan holds <strong>over 88% to 89% of India's total lead-zinc geological reserves</strong> (~630 million tonnes of ore resources). Small remaining resources occur in Andhra Pradesh (Agnigundala in Guntur), Madhya Pradesh, and Bihar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Production:</strong> Rajasthan accounts for <strong>virtually 100% of India's total mined lead and zinc ore and refined primary metal production!</strong></li>\n                <li><strong>Operating Monopoly:</strong> All commercial lead-zinc mining, beneficiation, and smelting in India is operated by <strong>Hindustan Zinc Limited (HZL)</strong>, a subsidiary of Vedanta Limited (in which the Government of India retains a ~29.5% public equity stake).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Rampura Agucha (Bhilwara District): The Global Titan</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>World’s Premier Zinc Mine:</strong> Located in the Bhilwara district of southern Rajasthan. Discovered in 1980, Rampura Agucha is recognized by global mining bodies as the <strong>world's largest high-grade underground zinc-lead mine</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Extraordinary Ore Grade:</strong> Unlike typical global zinc mines which grade 4% to 6%, Rampura Agucha possesses a phenomenal average ore grade of <strong>14% to 15% combined metal content ($Zn + Pb$)</strong>, making it one of the lowest-cost and most lucrative zinc deposits on Earth.</li>\n                <li><strong>State-of-the-Art Underground Infrastructure:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Originally mined as a massive open-pit (over 380 meters deep).</li>\n                        <li>Successfully transitioned into an ultra-modern underground operation accessed via a <strong>950-meter deep, 6-meter diameter concrete-lined vertical hoisting shaft</strong> equipped with an automated friction winder hoisting 3.75 million tonnes of ore annually.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
