            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Historic Zawar Mines: The Birthplace of Global Zinc Metallurgy</h3>\n            <p>Located in the Girwa tehsil of Udaipur district, Rajasthan, the <strong>Zawar mining complex</strong> occupies a revered place in the history of global science and technology. Modern archaeo-metallurgical excavations have proved conclusively that <strong>India was the first country in the world to master the smelting and condensation of pure metallic zinc on an industrial scale</strong>, centuries before Europe!</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Scientific Problem of Zinc Smelting</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Thermodynamic Paradox:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Zinc oxide ($ZnO$) can only be chemically reduced to metallic zinc by carbon at temperatures above <strong>1,000°C</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>However, metallic zinc has an extraordinarily low boiling point of <strong>907°C</strong>!</li>\n                        <li>Therefore, as soon as zinc metal is formed in a standard furnace, it immediately vaporizes into zinc gas. If air is present, the hot gas instantly re-oxidizes back into worthless white zinc oxide dust ('Philosopher's Wool').</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Ancient Zawar Breakthrough: Downward Condensation Retorts</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Between the 12th and 14th centuries AD (and possibly as early as 400 BC), master metallurgists at Zawar solved this thermodynamic paradox by inventing the <strong>downward retort condensation furnace</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Method:</strong> Zinc ore (sphalerite/calamine), charcoal, and organic flux were packed inside thousands of sealed conical clay crucibles (retorts). The retorts were inverted upside-down in a brick furnace, with their open condensation necks protruding downwards through a perforated furnace floor into a cooler lower chamber.</li>\n                <li>When the furnace was fired from above, zinc vapor formed inside the sealed retort and distilled downward, condensing as liquid metallic zinc droplets into ceramic receiver vessels below without ever coming into contact with air!</li>\n                <li><strong>Global Historical Recognition:</strong> In 1988, the <em>American Society for Metals (ASM International)</em> and British metallurgical bodies awarded the Zawar Mines a prestigious <strong>International Historic Chemical Landmark plaque</strong>, officially acknowledging Zawar as the earliest industrial zinc distillation site in the world.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Modern Zawar Mining Operations</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Today, Zawar remains an active, modern underground mining complex operated by HZL, encompassing four contiguous mines: <strong>Mochia, Balaria, Zawarmala, and Baroi</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
