            "id": "m14",
            "title": "Gold Mineralogy & Indian Reserves: Primary Lode Reefs vs Placer Gold",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Gold Mineralogy & Indian Geological Endowments: Lode Reefs vs Alluvial Placers</h3>\n            <p><strong>Gold ($Au$)</strong> is a noble precious metal that has captivated human civilization for millennia. Known for its incorruptibility, high electrical conductivity, extreme malleability (a single ounce can be beaten into a 300-square-foot sheet of gold leaf), and universal monetary store of value, gold holds an unmatched emotional, cultural, and financial place in Indian society.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Mineralogy & Occurrence Modes</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Native Gold:</strong> Occurs almost exclusively as a native element ($Au$), typically alloyed with 5% to 15% silver (<em>Electrum</em>). It is chemically unreactive, insoluble in ordinary acids, and dissolves only in <strong>Aqua Regia</strong> (a 3:1 mixture of hydrochloric and nitric acids) or alkaline cyanide solutions.</li>\n                <li><strong>Primary Lode Deposits (Hydrothermal Quartz Reefs):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Gold is deposited by deep-seated hydrothermal fluids ascending through fractures in ancient greenstone schist belts (Archean Dharwar Craton). Gold particles are encapsulated inside milky quartz veins and associated with iron sulfides (pyrite, arsenopyrite).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Secondary Placer Deposits (Alluvial Gold):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Because native gold is chemically inert and has an extraordinarily high specific gravity (19.3 when pure!), it resists chemical weathering. When gold-bearing quartz veins erode, dense gold flakes and nuggets settle along river gravels, sandbars, and alluvial terraces.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Iconic Indian Gold Rivers:</strong>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Subarnarekha River ('Streak of Gold'):</strong> Rises near Ranchi in Jharkhand; indigenous tribal communities (Jhoras) historically pan river sands for gold particles.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Son River ('Golden River'):</strong> Originating on the Amarkantak plateau, famous since antiquity for alluvial placer gold.</li>\n                                <li>Ramganga, Sindh, and Dras rivers in the Western Himalayas.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Gold Reserves & Inferred Resources in India</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>According to the National Mineral Inventory (UNFC system), India has approximately 500 million tonnes of gold ore resources containing ~650 tonnes of metallic gold.</li>\n                <li><strong>State-Wise Share of Total Resources:</strong>\n                    <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:8px 0;\">\n                        Bihar (~44%) &gt; Rajasthan (~25%) &gt; Karnataka (~21%) &gt; West Bengal (~3%)\n                    </div>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Bihar's Jamui Endowment:</strong> In 2021–22, GSI surveys confirmed that the <strong>Sonu block in Jamui district of Bihar</strong> holds India's largest single inferred resource of gold ore (estimated at ~222 million tonnes of low-grade gold-bearing rock containing ~37 tonnes of metal).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Crucial Reserve vs Resource Distinction:</strong> While Bihar and Rajasthan lead in inferred, unmined <em>resources</em>, <strong>Karnataka holds the vast majority of economically proven, actively mineable <em>reserves</em></strong>!</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Native gold occurs as free metal or alloyed with silver (Electrum), dissolving only in Aqua Regia or cyanide.",
                "Primary gold occurs in hydrothermal quartz reefs within Archean greenstone schist belts (Dharwar).",
                "Secondary placer gold occurs in river gravels due to gold's high specific gravity (19.3).",
                "The Subarnarekha ('Streak of Gold') and Son rivers are historically famous for alluvial placer panning.",
                "Bihar holds ~44% of India's inferred gold ore resources (Sonu block in Jamui district).",
                "While Bihar and Rajasthan hold the largest resources, Karnataka holds the active mineable reserves."
            ],
