            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Gold Production in India: Karnataka's Monopoly, Hutti & The Legend of KGF</h3>\n            <p>India is the world’s largest or second-largest consumer of physical gold, importing between <strong>700 and 1,000 tonnes of gold bullion annually</strong> (costing over $35–$50 billion in foreign exchange). In contrast to this colossal domestic demand, India’s domestic primary mined gold production is tiny (less than <strong>1.5 tonnes per year</strong>). However, virtually all of this domestic production comes from a single state: <strong>Karnataka accounts for over 99% of India's gold output!</strong></p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Hutti Gold Mines (Raichur District, Karnataka)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>India's Sole Active Commercial Primary Gold Producer:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Located in the Raichur district of northern Karnataka within the <strong>Hutti-Maski Greenstone Belt</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Operated by the state government enterprise <strong>The Hutti Gold Mines Limited (HGML)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Accounts for <strong>over 99% of India’s total primary mined gold production</strong>, producing approximately 1,400 to 1,600 kg of refined gold bullion annually.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Underground Engineering:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Mined through deep vertical shafts including the <em>Mallappa Shaft</em> and <em>Central Shaft</em>, currently operating at depths over 800 to 900 meters.</li>\n                        <li>The run-of-mine ore typically grades around <strong>3.5 to 4.5 grams of gold per tonne of rock (g/t)</strong>. Extracted ore is milled, treated with carbon-in-pulp (CIP) cyanide leaching, electrowon, and smelted into gold doré bars on site.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Legend of Kolar Gold Fields (KGF - Champion Reef)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Historical Stature:</strong> Located in the Kolar district of southeastern Karnataka. Mined for over a thousand years (from the Ganga and Chola dynasties to Tipu Sultan). Developed industrially in 1880 by the British mining firm <strong>John Taylor & Sons</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Engineering Marvels at KGF:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>One of the Deepest Mines on Earth:</strong> The <strong>Champion Reef mine</strong> descended to an astonishing depth of <strong>3,211 meters (over 10,500 feet / 3.2 kilometers below surface)</strong>, making it the second deepest underground mine in the world at the time (after Western Deep Levels in South Africa)!</li>\n                        <li><strong>First Hydroelectric Power in Asia (1902):</strong> To power the massive electric hoists, ventilation compressors, and pumps at KGF, the Maharaja of Mysore and John Taylor & Sons commissioned Asia’s first major hydro-electric generating station at <strong>Shivanasamudra Falls on the Cauvery River in 1902</strong>, transmitting high-voltage power over 147 km to KGF!</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Decline & Closure (2001):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Operated post-nationalization by public sector enterprise <strong>Bharat Gold Mines Limited (BGML)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>As depth exceeded 3 km, rock temperatures surpassed <strong>65°C</strong>, requiring giant subterranean refrigeration air-conditioning plants. Frequent catastrophic rock bursts, declining ore grades, and escalating pumping costs forced the <strong>permanent closure of KGF in 2001</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Other Gold Fields: Ramagiri & Jonnagiri</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Ramagiri Gold Field (Anantapur, AP):</strong> Historic lode belt operated by BGML until 2001.</li>\n                <li><strong>Jonnagiri Gold Project (Kurnool, AP):</strong> Developed by private company Geomysore Services India, entering trial production.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
