            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>National Critical Minerals Strategy: 30 Critical Minerals, MMDR 2023 & KABIL</h3>\n            <p>In response to accelerating geopolitical weaponization of mineral supply chains and the urgent imperatives of the green energy transition, the Government of India rolled out a landmark structural overhaul of its national mineral strategy in 2023.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Identification of 30 Critical Minerals (June 2023)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The Ministry of Mines released a landmark expert committee report officially designating <strong>30 Critical Minerals</strong> essential for India’s economic growth, national defense, and net-zero clean energy goals:\n                    <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:8px 0;\">\n                        Antimony, Beryllium, Bismuth, Cobalt, Copper, Gallium, Germanium, Graphite, Hafnium, Indium, Lithium, Molybdenum, Niobium, Nickel, PGE, Phosphorus, Potash, REE, Rhenium, Selenium, Silicon, Tantalum, Tellurium, Tin, Titanium, Tungsten, Vanadium, Zirconium, Cadmium, and Glauconite.\n                    </div>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2023: Revolutionary Legal Reforms</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>De-listing Atomic Minerals for Private Mining:</strong> The Act removed <strong>6 atomic minerals</strong> from the restricted atomic list—<strong>Lithium, Beryllium, Niobium, Titanium, Tantalum, and Zirconium</strong>—enabling private commercial entities to bid for, explore, and mine them for the first time in Indian history!</li>\n                <li><strong>Central Government Auctioning Powers:</strong> The Central Government was granted direct statutory authority to exclusively conduct competitive e-auctions for critical and strategic mineral concessions across all states, accelerating mine operationalization.</li>\n                <li><strong>Exploration Licence (EL):</strong> Introduced a novel <em>Exploration Licence</em> mechanism to attract global junior mining exploration firms to discover deep-seated and concealed critical mineral deposits.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Khanij Bidesh India Limited (KABIL): Securing Overseas Assets</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Corporate Structure:</strong> Incorporated in 2019 as a strategic joint venture between three premier CPSEs under the Ministry of Mines:\n                    <br><strong>NALCO (40%) • HCL (30%) • MECL (30%)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Strategic Mandate:</strong> Identify, acquire, explore, develop, and import critical and strategic minerals (primarily Lithium and Cobalt) from mineral-rich overseas jurisdictions to secure India's supply chains.</li>\n                <li><strong>Historic Argentina Lithium Deal (January 2024):</strong> KABIL signed a landmark agreement with state-owned CAMYEN in <strong>Catamarca province, Argentina</strong>, securing exploration and exclusive commercial development rights across <strong>5 lithium brine blocks</strong> covering 15,700 hectares—India's first-ever government-to-government lithium acquisition!</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
